{"author":{"id":"8022379585b4ab99ca8775db3eb67d33fcb256817114c28e51ccc1e9f55cdec4"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1792269605,"nonce":"RJeR3aAI+XvuTMWDrwObOMNPtgU=","title":"rayzor run cold-start floor: ~17 ms before main, most of it mapping and binding libLLVM","message":"NUC, empty program (`trace(1)`), `rayzor run --release`, warm caches, P-cores:\n- 27 ms -\u003e 17 ms after 7e428ba5. The jemalloc LD_PRELOAD re-exec cost ~10 ms, and jemalloc is now linked in.\n- Remaining profile: kernel 48% (exec, mmap, page faults; about 5000 faults), ld-linux 27% (symbol lookup/relocation, mostly libLLVM.so 138 MB, 14405 relocations), rayzor 13% (stdlib mapping tables built from scratch: BTreeMap/SipHash inserts of runtime method signatures, inventory symbol registration, TypeInfo map), libLLVM 8%.\n- hxcpp deltablue is 8 ms end to end, so `rayzor run` cannot beat it cold while LLVM is mapped and bound at startup. The AOT binary is the like-for-like comparison.\n\nLevers:\n1. Load LLVM lazily, dlopen'd when the first function is promoted to the LLVM tier. Most short programs never get there. Needs the LLVM backend behind a dynamic boundary.\n2. Build the runtime-mapping tables once at build time (a static table or the snapshot), not with BTreeMap/HashMap inserts on every start.\n3. Fewer page faults: a smaller binary (strip, split debuginfo), and prelink-style relro.","files":null}]}